    "noteTexts" : {
      "948CBC94-2245-4F72-A77B-2BFA01A02FE2" : "# Release Findings Ledger\n\nCycle: not started\nScope: set by the Conductor at kickoff\n\n## Code Quality (Burnish)\n- no findings yet\n\n## Security (Palisade)\n- no findings yet\n\n## Correctness (Ferret)\n- no findings yet\n\n## UX & Accessibility (Lumen)\n- no findings yet\n\n## Conductor verdict\n- pending",
      "BDF52FAD-5FCA-44FD-BACB-934C94D85B31" : "# Release Readiness Playbook\n\n## Mission\nVerify work before it ships. Reviewers report findings, the Conductor decides, fixes happen only on explicit request from the user.\n\n## Severity scale\n- BLOCKER: must fix before release. Security holes, data loss, broken core flows, hard accessibility failures on critical paths.\n- MAJOR: should fix. Shipping with it is a conscious decision, not an accident.\n- MINOR: real issue, does not block the release.\n- NIT: polish, style, taste.\n\n## Finding format\nOne line per finding, inside your own section of the release-findings note:\n- [SEVERITY] path\/to\/file:line - what is wrong. Suggested fix.\n\n## How we collaborate\n1. The Conductor scopes the review and briefs each reviewer with a self-contained task.\n2. Reviewers ask back instead of guessing: maestri ask \"<requester>\" \"<question>\".\n3. Cross-lane handoffs: if you find something outside your specialty, maestri ask the right teammate directly.\n4. Everyone logs findings in release-findings, then reports a short summary and verdict back to the requester.\n5. The Conductor reads the full ledger and writes the final call in the Conductor verdict section.\n\n## Verdicts\nEach reviewer ends with exactly one: SHIP, SHIP WITH NITS, NEEDS WORK, DO NOT SHIP."
    },
